if expects any scalar type, which means all arithmetic types and
pointers. Basically, if it can be compared for equality to 0 at all, if
allows it; and if(value) evaluates, in all cases, the same as
if(value!=0). The introduction of _Bool didn't change any of that.

> And how will  _Bool c=42;  be handled?

To quote the Standard:

# When any scalar value is converted to _Bool, the result is 0 if the
# value compares equal to 0; otherwise, the result is 1.

